can you just talk to us a little bit  about you know the typical iceland  shopper and how difficult they're  finding things now  well the reality is there is no uh  typical icing shopper averages don't  work very well because we have people  who come in and spend five quid and  people who come in and spend 50 quid um  but yeah generally i think it's safe to  say that everyone is is feeling the  pinch now doesn't matter who you are and  now we we do serve um some of the poorer  demographics around the country and we  are hearing stories as you said of some  um some of our custom disappearing to  food banks which is a reality or indeed  some customers um when they're at the  till asking the cashier when it amounts  to 40 quid so that they can leave the  rest of their shopping um we're seeing  uh people trading to the best value um  packs and and products um so yeah i  think it's it's fair to say that  everyone's feeling the pinch but  certainly the harder press communities  are feeling it more than anyone  yeah it really does take a picture of  just how difficult so many people are  finding it and of course inflation a big  concern over the board but grocery price  uh inflation uh in particular a real  worry as they hit a 13-year high um can  you just give us a bit of a sense about  what specific foods so  arising by and how much  yeah um again it's you know there's  there's no one particular metric what's  interesting about that food inflation  figure um the median kind of food  inflation figure of seven eight percent  um it includes things like champagne and  legs of lamb um now some of our  customers certainly aren't buying that  and um the reality is that you know the  basic staples are increasing more we've  seen as you'll know loaf of bread which  is now 1 pound 10 it probably was about  89 p  milk  across all retailers has has gone up  quite dramatically it's across the board  and that's because there's a perfect  kind of maelstrom of inflationary  pressures from commodity um price  increases  through to labor shortages in in the  fields and factories um through to the  price of fuel to uh to move goods around  uh through to genuine supply shortages  like um  sunflower oil  or wheat and both of which come from  ukraine and for obvious reasons you know  both of those uh things have have dried  up and the unintended consequences of  that it's not just a bottle of sunflower  oil or the wheat in your bread um  sunflower oil finds its way as a  vegetable fat into hundreds of different  ready meals and pre-prepared meals  and wheat goes into animal feed so that  is having a knock-on consequence of the  price of everything from bacon to  chicken  it's interesting talking about ukraine  and the prime minister and president  zolensky spoke just a couple of days ago  about the impact of the blockage of the  port of odessa and the supply of of  grain around the world  and  just looking forward how concerned are  you about the impact that this could  have and how much do you think food  could actually go up by in the months  ahead  look um you know  there's people far cleverer than me who  are forecasting this stuff um i think  it's impossible to say i think from  where we are at the moment i'm not  gravely concerned around supply issues  uh i and i think if you look at our own  business we are especially resilient as  a frozen  first retailer um we we can build up  stocks we have uh long uh supply lines  which um which we've been working on two  years worth of shelf life so we're we're  very resilient and as a private family  business we can invest in the right  places for our customers and that's why  we're doing a ton of different offers  and deals and promotions and value  packages just to try and help through  our customers  last week for example on tuesday we ran  our first over 60s discount of 10  which was unbelievably well received um  by our um some of our customers and  we've held the price of all of our one  pound lines to the end of the year  that's a major investment by us because  the price the cost of those goods have  gone up so we're making no money on  those now but we think it's the right  thing to do  and we've reduced the threshold of our  home delivery because that service is  more important than ever for people who  can't afford to drive into town centers  and spend the money on fuel  and rather to take their free home  delivery service so there's loads of  different things that we can do it's  certainly not going to get any easier i  don't know how how much worse it's going  to get um but hopefully  ourselves and our competitors are  resilient enough to to get through it  for our customers  yeah certainly some concerning times  ahead the government unveiled its big  support package  this week to help families is there  anything specifically that you would  like to see the government do going  forward to try and help tackle you know  the rising cost of food  do you know what i must say you know i  think the government the chancellor in  particular has um  has really uh stepped up and and  you know pulled the right levers  they already had over 20 billion pounds  of support before this additional 20  billion pounds of latest packages so  certainly huge intervention there by the  government and actually we've got to  take a step back and realize that you  know a lot of these pressures are global  in nature and um a lot of these  pressures are outside of uh the  influence of of the government of the  day or businesses like ours so i think  they've they've they've done plenty  actually for the general public and  hopefully it will ease the pain and the  burden as we get into the autumn as as  um inflation may peak and there's a ton  of stuff i'd like him to do for business  um i don't know how he's going to  afford it um or we're all going to pay  for it um but i think at the moment  they're doing a good job

